"Models strategic choices in their move order while showing which histories each player can distinguish.\n\nUse when: Study negotiation, market entry or commitment decisions. Explain how observability changes a rival's response options.\n\nAvoid when: All choices are simultaneous and their timing adds no relevant information.\n\nVisual grammar: Decision nodes identify the moving player; branches identify available actions. Leaves contain ordered player payoffs. Information sets group indistinguishable decision histories without implying a move.\n\nBuild: List players and the sequence of possible actions. Mark observations and connect indistinguishable histories as information sets. Assign terminal consequences and analyze credible continuation choices.\n\nCheck: Information sets contain nodes with consistent available actions. Each path has well-defined player payoffs. Backward induction is used only where its information assumptions apply.\n\nPitfalls: Confusing an information-set connector with a playable action. Treating a noncredible future threat as a committed strategy.\n\nExample: A new entrant chooses enter or stay out; an incumbent observes entry and chooses accommodation or a price response.\n\nAlternatives: Payoff matrix, Decision tree."
